=== Scene 5/8: A survey firm reads the open call and ticks the area it can staff
  · goto https://labs.connect.dimagi.com/solicitations/4663/respond/?program_id=10008
  · before-frame scene_5_before.png
    · scroll_to(css:#sol-plan-checklist)  — NON-effecting camera move scrolling the "Which areas can you cover?" checklist into frame. CRITICAL for the before-frame — because this leading action is NOT a wait_for, the recorder runs the full domcontentloaded + load + goto_settle_ms + initial_hold_ms flow, so the /respond/ form is fully painted and settled (checklist visible, Submit disabled — UNCHECKED state) before the before-frame fires. With a leading wait_for the navigation used wait_until=commit and the before-frame caught the crossfading-out owner manage view instead of this respond form.
    · wait_for(css:#sol-plan-checklist input[type=checkbox])  — gate on the coverage CHECKLIST checkbox — the load-bearing, always-present DOM element that proves the respond page (for the just-minted call) has loaded. This is the navigation gate; we do NOT gate on the Mapbox canvas here because the WebGL canvas paints later/asynchronously and a slow canvas must not block the (reliable) checklist click.
    · wait_for(css:#sol-respond-map canvas.mapboxgl-canvas)  — best-effort wait (≤5s) for the coverage map to paint the Attakar (green) + Gura (blue) ward polygons — NON-blocking; if the WebGL canvas is slow this logs a timeout and we proceed, since the checklist (already present) is what the click needs. Capped at 5s so a slow canvas adds at most 5s of dead air, not 12s.
    · hold()  — let the map settle and fit to the two wards — the form is now framed in its UNCHECKED state (Submit disabled); the next action ticks the coverage area, so the before/after pair shows the checklist-commit mechanic end-to-end (disabled → submitted)
    · scroll_to(css:#id_firm_name)  — bring the "Your organization" identity fields into frame — a real firm names itself when it answers a public call; the owner reviews and awards a NAMED survey firm, not the individual account that submitted the form
    · fill(css:#id_firm_name)  — the applying firm names itself — Sahel Field Research, a plausible northern-Nigeria independent household-survey firm. This is what renders on the responses list and award screen (org_name), so scenes 4-5 show a believable firm, NOT the operator's own ace@dimagi-ai.com dev account.
    · fill(css:#id_firm_email)  — the firm's contact email — stored as submitted_by_email and shown beside the firm name on the responses list, replacing the operator account's email
    · scroll_to(css:#sol-plan-checklist)  — scroll back to the coverage checklist now that the firm has identified itself, to commit the area
    · click(css:#sol-plan-checklist input[type=checkbox])  — tick the coverage area — synced two-way with the map; this enables Submit (≥1 required). The created call has exactly one plan (R6 — Attakar × Gura), so the first checkbox is that area.
    · scroll_to(css:textarea[name^="question_"])  — the application question Maya set — with the AI-generated scoring criteria shown right beside it
    · hold()  — the firm sees exactly how it will be scored (criteria visible to respondents)
    · fill(css:textarea[name^="question_"])  — the firm drafts its answer
